
Building Charm++ shared library for Charm4py
============================================

- Build libcharm using the 'charm4py' build target.
  For example, on a regular Linux machine:
  ./build charm4py netlrts-linux-x86_64 -j8 --with-production
  or, on the reconverse runtime:
  ./build charm4py reconverse-linux-x86_64 -j8 --with-production

  The target builds Charm++ as a shared library (libcharm) and defines
  CMK_LIBCHARM_SHARED=1 in conv-autoconfig.h. A binding such as charm4py
  loads the library at run time and drives it through the CkDyn layer
  (src/ck-core/ckdyn.h; developer documentation in doc/internals/ckdyn.md,
  user documentation in doc/ckdyn/manual.rst).

  NOTE: We are currently requiring non-smp mode because in the most common Python implementation
  (CPython), multiple threads cannot run Python code concurrently due to the Global Interpreter Lock
  (GIL).

Adding modules (e.g. load balancers) to libcharm
------------------------------------------------

There are modules like tracing and load balancers that the Charm++ runtime expects
to be registered by code that charmc generates when it links an executable
(moduleinit<pid>.C): CkRegisterMainModule, _registerExternalModules and
_createTraces. A shared libcharm has no such executable, so:

- init.C defines a weak _createTraces (with the projections and summary
  traces in a CMK_LIBCHARM_SHARED build with tracing enabled);
- init.C registers TreeLB and the traces itself in a CMK_LIBCHARM_SHARED
  build (the section of _initCharm under #if CMK_LIBCHARM_SHARED); a module
  added to libcharm needs its _register call added there, and the
  LIBCHARM_LIBS variable in the Makefile needs to include the module;
- in place of CkRegisterMainModule, the binding registers its classes through
  the CkDyn hook table (CkDynInit before CkDynStart).

The older charm4py 1.x interface (the "Ext API": ReadOnlyExt, MainchareExt,
GroupExt, ArrayElemExt, the Ck*Ext functions and the register*ExtCallback
family) has been removed; CkDyn replaces it.
